From d18de766b072b9023a4eed5214d8f6c39cd39499 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 09:38:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: S7 -- fix AUDIOPRB.LOG producing no real-hardware
 data

WHY: the S7 real-hardware re-run (2026-09-14,
tests/games/trilbysnotes/results/S7-fix-realhw-2026-09-14.md, "A second
open question") found patch 0059's own AUDIOPRB.LOG diagnostic never
appended anything during either 308s/309s real sound-on run on
HW-486-66, despite genuine MIDI activity (4980/4976 events dispatched
per audio_vital_status) -- the only line ever recovered came from the
very first cell of that four-cell A/B/B/A block and nothing after it,
in that cell or any later one, ever added another.

The one thing in this engine's own log writing PROVEN reliable across
all four of that same real-hardware block's cells is quit.cpp's own
RUNMANIFEST-tag log: a single fopen(tag,"a") kept open across several
fprintf()s and closed exactly once at clean shutdown -- never reopened
mid-run. AUDIOPRB.LOG's original design was the opposite: a fresh
fopen("AUDIOPRB.LOG","a")/fprintf/fclose cycle every
AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_WINDOW (200) calls, repeatedly reopening the same
already-populated file over a single run and across every cell of one
boot session. That repeated-reopen pattern is the one structural
difference from the write pattern that worked every time, so this
converges the diagnostic onto the proven pattern instead of guessing at
the exact underlying DOS/FAT mechanism.

WHAT: audio_core.cpp's window-close routine (renamed
dos_diag_close_window(), was dos_diag_flush_audio_poll()) no longer does
any file I/O -- it appends the closed window's summary into a small
bounded in-memory array (AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_MAX_WINDOWS=64, well above the
low thousands of calls DOSAGS_TAS_AUTO_EXIT_TICK bounds any S7 cell to)
instead. A new dos_diag_dump_audio_poll(FILE *fp), declared in
audio_core.h under __DJGPP__, folds in the still-open partial window and
writes every recorded window as "DOS-PORT audioprb ..." lines through a
caller-supplied, already-open FILE* -- it never opens or closes anything
itself. quit.cpp calls it with its own run_manifest_log handle, right
before that handle's single fclose(), same DJGPP-gated block as the
existing memory_min_free_kb/tick_rate lines already use for exactly this
reason.

DOSBox-X validated (dosbox-run.sh, oplmode=opl3 scratch conf, Trilby's
Notes, DOSAGS_FIXED_TIMESTEP=1, DOSAGS_TAS_AUTO_EXIT_TICK=200,
--user-data-dir c:\ per PLAN.md's own check_write_access() note): a real
SB16 device opened, audio_core_entry_poll() was called 69 times, and the
tag log now carries "DOS-PORT audioprb total_calls=69 windows_recorded=1
windows_dropped=0" plus the window's own "slots_us(...) pump_us(min=460
mean=10097 max=14317)" line -- the pump_us mean matches the ~10-11ms/call
this same investigation already measured on DOSBox-X via the original
(working, there) sink, confirming the new sink reproduces the same data,
not just that it runs. This is NOT yet a real-hardware confirmation that
the fix resolves the actual gap -- DOSBox-X already proved unreliable
once this same investigation for predicting real-hardware audio timing
behavior (S7-fix-realhw-2026-09-14.md) -- only that the new sink is wired
correctly end to end and produces the expected shape of data. A real
HW-486-66 sound-on cell is still required before trusting this.

Still temporary, same as 0059 -- to be reverted once S7's investigation
concludes and both diagnostics are no longer needed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 Engine/main/quit.cpp              |   9 +++
 Engine/media/audio/audio_core.cpp | 126 ++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 Engine/media/audio/audio_core.h   |  11 +++
 3 files changed, 121 insertions(+), 25 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Engine/main/quit.cpp b/Engine/main/quit.cpp
index 5c60d76d8..9485b11fd 100644
--- a/Engine/main/quit.cpp
+++ b/Engine/main/quit.cpp
@@ -31,6 +31,7 @@
 #ifdef __DJGPP__
 #include "runmanifest.h"
 #include "platform/dos/dos_audio_mixer.h"
+#include "media/audio/audio_core.h" // dos_diag_dump_audio_poll() -- TEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC, see audio_core.cpp
 #endif
 
 #include "platform/platform.h"
@@ -419,6 +420,14 @@ void quit(const char *quitmsg)
                     tick_rate_hz, dos_get_frames_skipped());
             }
 
+            // TEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC (S7): dump the AUDIOPRB window data
+            // (audio_core.cpp) through THIS handle rather than a separate
+            // periodically-reopened AUDIOPRB.LOG -- see that file's own
+            // comment on why the original separate-file sink produced no
+            // data on real hardware and this one reuses the pattern proven
+            // reliable across every S7 real-hardware cell instead.
+            dos_diag_dump_audio_poll(run_manifest_log);
+
             fclose(run_manifest_log);
         }
     }
diff --git a/Engine/media/audio/audio_core.cpp b/Engine/media/audio/audio_core.cpp
index 07351f569..0dc753b4c 100644
--- a/Engine/media/audio/audio_core.cpp
+++ b/Engine/media/audio/audio_core.cpp
@@ -213,21 +213,62 @@ extern uclock_t dos_port_engine_start_uclock;
 // that, directly at the two call sites named in the writeup's own "not
 // yet done" section.
 //
-// Same in-memory-accumulate, periodic-flush idiom as .sdl-dos-ports/
-// shared/patches/sdl3-dos/0136 -- that investigation independently
-// measured a per-call fopen/fprintf/fclose (even "every 20 frames") to
-// cost a real ~0.4-0.5fps, so this does no I/O on the hot path either.
-// Stats are per-window (reset every AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_WINDOW calls, not
-// cumulative-since-start) so a shift in behavior over the run (e.g. once
-// the fixed-timestep backlog maxes out) is visible rather than averaged
-// away; window size is a call count, not a time interval, since this
-// function's own call RATE is the thing under suspicion.
+// S7 real-hardware re-run (2026-09-14,
+// tests/games/trilbysnotes/results/S7-fix-realhw-2026-09-14.md, "A second
+// open question") found this diagnostic's ORIGINAL sink -- a periodic
+// fopen("AUDIOPRB.LOG","a")/fprintf/fclose cycle, repeated every
+// AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_WINDOW calls -- produced exactly ONE line on real
+// hardware across a whole four-cell A/B/B/A block (one successful flush,
+// from the very first cell; every flush after that, in that same cell and
+// every cell after it, wrote nothing), while DOSBox-X flushed the same
+// pattern cleanly every time. The one thing in this engine's own log
+// writing that is PROVEN reliable across all four of that same block's
+// real-hardware cells is quit.cpp's RUNMANIFEST-tag log: a single
+// fopen(tag,"a") kept open for several fprintf()s and closed exactly once
+// at clean shutdown -- never reopened mid-run. That is the one structural
+// difference between the write pattern that worked every time and the one
+// that broke after its first success, so this fix converges onto it
+// instead of guessing at the underlying DOS/FAT mechanism: stop opening
+// AUDIOPRB.LOG at all, accumulate every window's summary in a small
+// bounded in-memory array instead of flushing to disk immediately, and
+// dump the whole array as extra lines through quit.cpp's own already-open
+// run_manifest_log handle (dos_diag_dump_audio_poll(), audio_core.h) right
+// before that handle's own single fclose(). This still needs a real-
+// hardware re-run to confirm the data actually lands this time -- treat
+// the mechanism above as the best-supported hypothesis from the evidence
+// on hand, not a confirmed root cause.
+//
+// Same in-memory-accumulate idiom as .sdl-dos-ports/shared/patches/
+// sdl3-dos/0136 -- that investigation independently measured a per-call
+// fopen/fprintf/fclose (even "every 20 frames") to cost a real ~0.4-0.5fps,
+// so this still does no I/O on the hot path. Stats are per-window (reset
+// every AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_WINDOW calls, not cumulative-since-start) so a
+// shift in behavior over the run is visible rather than averaged away;
+// window size is a call count, not a time interval, since this function's
+// own call RATE is the thing under suspicion.
+//
+// AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_MAX_WINDOWS bounds the array: DOSAGS_TAS_AUTO_EXIT_TICK
+// (the fixed tick cap every S7 cell runs under) keeps total real-tick
+// audio_core_entry_poll() calls in the low thousands at most, so 64
+// windows (12800 calls) is generous headroom; a run that somehow exceeds
+// it just stops recording NEW windows (total_calls keeps counting) rather
+// than overflowing or dropping early (more interesting, per the S1 catch-
+// up-burst history) windows to make room.
 //
 // Temporary -- to be reverted once S7's fix lands and re-measures clean,
 // same convention as 0136 itself.
 namespace {
 
 const uint32_t AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_WINDOW = 200;
+const uint32_t AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_MAX_WINDOWS = 64;
+
+struct AudioPollWindow
+{
+    uint32_t total_calls_at_close;
+    uint32_t window_calls;
+    uint64_t slots_min_us, slots_mean_us, slots_max_us;
+    uint64_t pump_min_us, pump_mean_us, pump_max_us;
+};
 
 struct AudioPollDiag
 {
@@ -235,6 +276,10 @@ struct AudioPollDiag
     uint32_t window_calls = 0;
     uint64_t slots_sum_us = 0, slots_min_us = UINT64_MAX, slots_max_us = 0;
     uint64_t pump_sum_us = 0, pump_min_us = UINT64_MAX, pump_max_us = 0;
+
+    AudioPollWindow windows[AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_MAX_WINDOWS];
+    uint32_t windows_recorded = 0;
+    uint32_t windows_dropped = 0;   // AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_MAX_WINDOWS exceeded
 };
 AudioPollDiag g_audio_poll_diag;
 
@@ -243,26 +288,29 @@ uint64_t dos_diag_now_us()
     return (uint64_t)((uclock() - dos_port_engine_start_uclock) * 1000000ULL / UCLOCKS_PER_SEC);
 }
 
-void dos_diag_flush_audio_poll()
+// Closes out the current in-progress window (if it has any calls) into the
+// in-memory array and resets the accumulators. No file I/O -- see the
+// block comment above for why this no longer opens AUDIOPRB.LOG itself.
+void dos_diag_close_window()
 {
     AudioPollDiag &d = g_audio_poll_diag;
     if (d.window_calls == 0)
         return;
-    FILE *fp = fopen("AUDIOPRB.LOG", "a");
-    if (fp)
+    if (d.windows_recorded < AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_MAX_WINDOWS)
     {
-        fprintf(fp,
-            "total_calls=%lu window_calls=%lu "
-            "slots_us(min=%lu mean=%lu max=%lu) "
-            "pump_us(min=%lu mean=%lu max=%lu)\n",
-            (unsigned long)d.total_calls, (unsigned long)d.window_calls,
-            (unsigned long)(d.slots_min_us == UINT64_MAX ? 0 : d.slots_min_us),
-            (unsigned long)(d.slots_sum_us / d.window_calls),
-            (unsigned long)d.slots_max_us,
-            (unsigned long)(d.pump_min_us == UINT64_MAX ? 0 : d.pump_min_us),
-            (unsigned long)(d.pump_sum_us / d.window_calls),
-            (unsigned long)d.pump_max_us);
-        fclose(fp);
+        AudioPollWindow &w = d.windows[d.windows_recorded++];
+        w.total_calls_at_close = d.total_calls;
+        w.window_calls = d.window_calls;
+        w.slots_min_us = (d.slots_min_us == UINT64_MAX ? 0 : d.slots_min_us);
+        w.slots_mean_us = d.slots_sum_us / d.window_calls;
+        w.slots_max_us = d.slots_max_us;
+        w.pump_min_us = (d.pump_min_us == UINT64_MAX ? 0 : d.pump_min_us);
+        w.pump_mean_us = d.pump_sum_us / d.window_calls;
+        w.pump_max_us = d.pump_max_us;
+    }
+    else
+    {
+        d.windows_dropped++;
     }
     d.window_calls = 0;
     d.slots_sum_us = 0; d.slots_min_us = UINT64_MAX; d.slots_max_us = 0;
@@ -281,10 +329,38 @@ void dos_diag_record_audio_poll(uint64_t slots_us, uint64_t pump_us)
     if (pump_us < d.pump_min_us) d.pump_min_us = pump_us;
     if (pump_us > d.pump_max_us) d.pump_max_us = pump_us;
     if (d.window_calls >= AUDIO_POLL_DIAG_WINDOW)
-        dos_diag_flush_audio_poll();
+        dos_diag_close_window();
 }
 
 } // anonymous namespace
+
+// Declared in audio_core.h (DJGPP-gated); called from quit.cpp right
+// before it closes its own RUNMANIFEST-tag log handle. `fp` is that
+// already-open handle -- this function never opens or closes anything
+// itself, deliberately, per the block comment above.
+void dos_diag_dump_audio_poll(FILE *fp)
+{
+    if (!fp)
+        return;
+    AudioPollDiag &d = g_audio_poll_diag;
+    // Fold the still-in-progress partial window in too, so a run that
+    // exits mid-window doesn't silently lose its tail.
+    dos_diag_close_window();
+    fprintf(fp, "DOS-PORT audioprb total_calls=%lu windows_recorded=%lu windows_dropped=%lu\n",
+        (unsigned long)d.total_calls, (unsigned long)d.windows_recorded,
+        (unsigned long)d.windows_dropped);
+    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < d.windows_recorded; i++)
+    {
+        const AudioPollWindow &w = d.windows[i];
+        fprintf(fp,
+            "DOS-PORT audioprb total_calls=%lu window_calls=%lu "
+            "slots_us(min=%lu mean=%lu max=%lu) "
+            "pump_us(min=%lu mean=%lu max=%lu)\n",
+            (unsigned long)w.total_calls_at_close, (unsigned long)w.window_calls,
+            (unsigned long)w.slots_min_us, (unsigned long)w.slots_mean_us, (unsigned long)w.slots_max_us,
+            (unsigned long)w.pump_min_us, (unsigned long)w.pump_mean_us, (unsigned long)w.pump_max_us);
+    }
+}
 #endif // __DJGPP__
 
 void audio_core_entry_poll()
diff --git a/Engine/media/audio/audio_core.h b/Engine/media/audio/audio_core.h
index 938b3a5d4..133f3b3e8 100644
--- a/Engine/media/audio/audio_core.h
+++ b/Engine/media/audio/audio_core.h
@@ -107,4 +107,15 @@ void audio_core_slot_stop(int slot_handle);
 void audio_core_entry_poll();
 #endif
 
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+#include <cstdio>
+// TEMPORARY DIAGNOSTIC (S7, see audio_core.cpp): dumps every in-memory
+// AUDIOPRB window recorded so far (plus the still-open partial window) as
+// "DOS-PORT audioprb ..." lines into an ALREADY-OPEN, caller-owned FILE*.
+// Called from quit.cpp right before it closes its own RUNMANIFEST-tag log
+// handle -- see that call site for why this piggybacks on that handle
+// instead of opening AUDIOPRB.LOG itself.
+void dos_diag_dump_audio_poll(FILE *fp);
+#endif
+
 #endif // __AGS_EE_MEDIA__AUDIOCORE_H
-- 
